WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.750
Wow, I am really surprised by this paper.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:09.519
You see, we know from an earlier paper that
in virtual worlds, glittery materials like

00:00:09.519 --> 00:00:16.379
this, and brushed aluminum surfaces like this
can be rendered with ray tracing based techniques.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:19.810
Light simulations on a computer, if you will.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:24.050
That is wonderful, but I am not surprised
by that.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.570
What I am surprised about is this paper.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.990
This one does something truly incredible.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:37.190
Hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars because
here is a simulated ceramic material with

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:45.140
multiple layers, with fingerprints, with dust,
with smudges, oh my goodness, I am going to

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:46.140
pass out.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.330
Look at that!

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:54.120
It can also simulate the glitter on the plastic
handle here, and the metal part of the cheese

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:59.960
slicer is simulated down to the tiniest little
scratches of the surface.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.060
Absolutely loving it.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:07.310
Note that these are all computer simulations
of virtual worlds.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.020
Not real photographs.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:15.229
This is beauty that only a proper computer
graphics research paper can offer.

00:01:15.229 --> 00:01:16.369
Unmatched.

00:01:16.369 --> 00:01:20.880
And for the teapot, the metal handle was also
something else.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:27.890
When I started my PhD in light transport simulation
in 2013, I did not think that we will be able

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:34.329
to do this kind of graphics in my lifetime,
and about 10 years later, here it is.

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:36.070
I can’t believe it.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:42.670
So, with this technique, we can capture real
materials, and layer by layer, put them into

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:49.530
a computer program where we can simulate them…well,
how quickly can we simulate them?

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:54.990
I bet we have to wait for hours for every
single image, right?

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.640
Well, not quite.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.950
Let me explain.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:02.710
This is a neural network-based technique,
where we take a stupendously large amount

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:09.009
of material reflectance data, for 40 billion
different training samples.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:11.870
And all this takes…wait, what?

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:18.069
4-5 hours per material to train on one commodity
graphics card?

00:02:18.069 --> 00:02:19.680
How is that even possible?

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.030
Well, let’s pop the hood and take a closer
look.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.030
Oh yes.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:31.990
The neural network is tiny, very lightweight
presumably for quick training and running,

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:35.099
we will have a look at that in a moment.

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:42.970
However, it is compressing an enormous amount
of data, that is, 2.5 billion latent parameters.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:49.290
And after the training is done, we can use
these materials quickly for as long as we

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.290
wish.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:56.409
Now, in summary, the neural network that is
behind this is a tiny, lightweight encoder

00:02:56.409 --> 00:03:03.239
that understands and compresses this enormous
amount of reflectance information down into

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:05.930
something smaller, more manageable.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.790
So, we have two more important questions left.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:14.090
One, is it as good as a true, reference simulation?

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.530
I can’t wait, let’s have a look together.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:21.459
This is what it looks like compared to the
reference simulation.

00:03:21.459 --> 00:03:23.330
Now, have a look here.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:29.480
You see, this difference does not mean that
the new technique is less sparkly, it’s

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:30.480
not worse.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:37.040
It is actually better, because it has less
noise, something that is inherent to real

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.930
light transport simulations.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:40.700
Ray tracers, if you will.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:46.970
You can witness it on a simple diffuse surface
too which is not meant to be sparkly at all.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:54.610
Wow, this means that it is nearly indistinguishable
from the true reference simulation, and is

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.180
even faster.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.319
So, how much faster?

00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:06.670
Well, get this, it is 2-10 times faster than
the reference solution, which is incredible.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:09.189
So, question number two.

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:11.650
What does this speed look like?

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:12.950
Well, look!

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:19.549
When we move the camera around in real time,
we get a really noisy image, but, look!

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:26.740
As we stop, we have more time to simulate
more rays to clean up this image, which happens

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.490
almost immediately.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:34.710
There is still noise in these images, but
don’t forget, we have many powerful noise

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:40.610
filtering techniques that are specifically
tailored for ray tracing algorithms, so I

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:47.199
am very hopeful that one or two more papers
down the line, and we will be able to do this

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:48.380
in real time.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:49.800
Yes, that’s right.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:56.170
I didn’t think this would be possible in
my lifetime, and through the power of computer

00:04:56.170 --> 00:05:04.009
graphics, AI research, and human ingenuity,
it is not only possible, but it will be possible

00:05:04.009 --> 00:05:05.770
in real time.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:06.770
Pinch me.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:09.699
What a time to be alive!

00:05:09.699 --> 00:05:38.520
This was Two Minute Papers with Dr. Károly
Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:06:10.819
Subscribe if you wish to see more.

